1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:03,040
<i>Like a fairy tale</i>

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:05,460
<i>It's a sign the story has reached its end</i>

3
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:08,380
<i>Like a single passage taken from</i>

4
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:10,000
<i>A journey that was too long</i>

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,340
<i>Of the evil that once cast a shadow</i>

6
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:14,680
<i>Across this land</i>

7
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:17,010
<i>And the memories of the short journey</i>

8
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:19,470
<i>With the hero who defeated it</i>

9
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:31,780
<i>The story ends</i>

10
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:34,280
<i>And the hero goes to sleep</i>

11
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:37,110
<i>Leaving this land with</i>

12
00:00:37,110 --> 00:00:38,780
<i>Days of peace and quiet</i>

13
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:41,290
<i>The passage of time heartlessly</i>

14
00:00:41,290 --> 00:00:43,450
<i>Makes people forget</i>

15
00:00:43,450 --> 00:00:46,250
<i>Even the tracks left behind by his life</i>

16
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:48,040
<i>Begin to rust</i>

17
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:49,880
<i>Just the same</i>

18
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:52,170
<i>Your words and wishes and courage</i>

19
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:55,510
<i>Are still inside of me</i>

20
00:00:55,510 --> 00:00:57,380
<i>And they live on</i>

21
00:00:58,180 --> 00:00:59,930
<i>We chose the same path</i>

22
00:00:59,930 --> 00:01:02,060
<i>That's all it was supposed to be</i>

23
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:03,600
<i>Why is it that at some point</i>

24
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:07,850
<i>I wanted to understand the reason for</i>

25
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:09,230
<i>The tears that fall down my cheeks</i>

26
00:01:09,230 --> 00:01:10,310
<i>Even now</i>

27
00:01:10,310 --> 00:01:12,690
<i>If I retrace the journey we walked together</i>

28
00:01:12,690 --> 00:01:16,240
<i>Even though you're not there</i>

29
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:17,910
<i>I know I'll be able to find it</i>

30
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:51,520
How long have you been traveling together?

31
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:54,350
Um...

32
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:58,900
I first embarked on my journey with
Ms. Frieren about four years ago.

33
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:01,290
It's been about a year for me.

34
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:05,350
I see.

35
00:02:10,290 --> 00:02:13,930
There's something this party lacks.

36
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:16,030
What do you think that is?

37
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:21,970
We've got a front-liner, mages, and a priest.

38
00:02:21,970 --> 00:02:23,450
A thief, perhaps?

39
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:26,110
Since Ms. Frieren is always
falling for mimics.

40
00:02:26,110 --> 00:02:27,560
Aw, yeah.

41
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:29,680
Oh, come on.

42
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:34,190
Treasure chests contain unlimited potential.

43
00:02:34,190 --> 00:02:35,260
No.

44
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:38,710
What we lack is something
even more important.

45
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:41,570
An older lady!

46
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:47,070
Most parties have at least
one sexy older lady!

47
00:02:47,710 --> 00:02:49,030
Do they?

48
00:02:49,890 --> 00:02:52,040
But we've got Frieren.

49
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:55,120
No. She's not an older lady.

50
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:56,740
<i>He looks so distraught.</i>

51
00:02:56,740 --> 00:02:58,040
Sorry about that.

52
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:02,000
H-Hey, the next village is coming into view.

53
00:03:19,670 --> 00:03:21,360
They're breathing.

54
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:23,320
Same over here.

55
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:27,320
What's going on here? Everyone's asleep.

56
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:32,220
This smells like trouble.

57
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:33,910
Let's head to the next village.

58
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:36,410
Don't make me angry, Ms. Frieren.

59
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:37,690
I'm just kidding.

60
00:03:39,230 --> 00:03:40,940
It's a curse.

61
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:43,570
I thought so. What a hassle.

62
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:47,480
Hey, Frieren. What are curses?

63
00:03:47,990 --> 00:03:51,090
Among the spells cast by monsters and demons

64
00:03:51,090 --> 00:03:55,110
are those that put people to sleep
or turn them to stone.

65
00:03:55,110 --> 00:04:01,140
Those which humankind has yet
to unravel are known as curses.

66
00:04:02,630 --> 00:04:06,620
Humanity's magic can't explain the principles
behind them or how to remove them.

67
00:04:07,470 --> 00:04:10,070
Does that mean it's too late for this village?

68
00:04:10,430 --> 00:04:13,610
I'm only talking about humanity's magic.

69
00:04:14,310 --> 00:04:17,830
The magic of the Goddess,
which priests use, is a different story.

70
00:04:18,730 --> 00:04:22,450
Tackling curses has long been
the business of the clergy.

71
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:25,840
Well, Sein? Think you can help them?

72
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:28,020
Don't rush me.

73
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:30,720
I'm currently determining the
type and source of the curse.

74
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:38,470
How is the magic of the Goddess
different from other magic?

75
00:04:38,830 --> 00:04:42,470
The magic of the Goddess
is recorded in holy scriptures

76
00:04:42,470 --> 00:04:44,990
and can only be used by
the owners of said scriptures.

77
00:04:45,450 --> 00:04:50,250
Like the demons' magic, we don't understand
the principles behind most of it,

78
00:04:50,590 --> 00:04:53,490
so it's not very interesting.

79
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,410
And it's difficult to use unless you're born
with the specific knack for it,

80
00:05:00,530 --> 00:05:04,500
which also bestows an
increased resistance to curses.

81
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:08,510
I believe they call it
"the blessing of the Goddess."

82
00:05:08,510 --> 00:05:12,010
Anyway, that's why priests
are strong against curses.

83
00:05:12,710 --> 00:05:16,320
So not even you can effectively
defend against curses?

84
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:19,730
Mages can't detect curses.

85
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:23,000
I can sense I'm being attacked in some way,

86
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,150
so I know this village is still under
the effects of the curse.

87
00:05:27,150 --> 00:05:28,680
I feel anxious.

88
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:32,990
Does that mean we're actively
being cursed? I'm scared!

89
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:37,750
I've determined that it's
a rather unusual curse.

90
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:39,760
You can't remove it?

91
00:05:40,070 --> 00:05:43,060
I'd need tools to conduct
a specialized ritual.

92
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:46,430
It'd be faster to defeat
the source of the curse.

93
00:05:47,530 --> 00:05:50,900
It's coming from a monster.
I've determined its location.

94
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:51,800
Let's go.

95
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:54,070
That'll save time.

96
00:05:57,570 --> 00:05:58,760
Ms. Frieren.

97
00:05:59,610 --> 00:06:00,940
It's Mr. Stark.

98
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:05,950
He's asleep.

99
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:09,470
Can you wake him temporarily?

100
00:06:09,470 --> 00:06:14,320
With my current abilities, five seconds
is the longest I can keep him awake.

101
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:16,240
That's practically nothing.

102
00:06:16,990 --> 00:06:19,770
In any case, it looks like we should hurry.

103
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:43,420
Fern won't wake up.

104
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:45,720
She said she was just taking a little break.

105
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:48,110
So Fern has fallen asleep, too.

106
00:06:48,830 --> 00:06:50,920
This is starting to look bad.

107
00:06:53,750 --> 00:06:55,400
I put up a barrier.

108
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:57,450
We'll hide them in here.

109
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:04,620
We're almost there. I can tell it's close.

110
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:06,920
I see.

111
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:10,270
Sein, wake me when the monster shows up.

112
00:07:11,370 --> 00:07:14,030
Don't fight it alone.

113
00:07:14,030 --> 00:07:16,000
I promise I'll defeat it.

114
00:07:16,420 --> 00:07:19,490
I told you, five seconds
is the longest I can...

115
00:07:21,220 --> 00:07:22,210
Frieren?

116
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:26,460
We can't do anything with five seconds.

117
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:36,290
Fine.

118
00:07:36,290 --> 00:07:39,070
I have battle magic, too.

119
00:07:57,450 --> 00:07:59,270
<i>It's a subspecies of chaos flower.</i>

120
00:07:59,630 --> 00:08:02,450
<i>It must be feeding off
the sleeping villagers.</i>

121
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:07,260
<i>It'll sap away their mana
until it eventually kills them.</i>

122
00:08:12,140 --> 00:08:17,570
<i>Chaos flowers merge with the
local flora to create subspecies.</i>

123
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:21,550
<i>It's like a mirror.</i>

124
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:27,530
<i>But no matter what they look like,
their weakness is always their core.</i>

125
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,870
Three Spears of the Goddess!

126
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:41,890
<i>What happened?</i>

127
00:08:42,320 --> 00:08:45,900
<i>Did it reflect my magic?</i>

128
00:08:46,730 --> 00:08:48,820
<i>Unless I score a direct hit on the core,</i>

129
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:52,950
<i>the leaves could deflect
my spells in any direction.</i>

130
00:08:54,060 --> 00:08:56,200
<i>If I'm not careful, it could hit Frieren.</i>

131
00:08:58,410 --> 00:09:01,080
<i>Damn it. This can only get worse.</i>

132
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:03,040
<i>Should I wake Frieren?</i>

133
00:09:03,410 --> 00:09:05,660
<i>But she's the mage from the party of heroes.</i>

134
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:07,660
<i>If a powerful spell were deflected,</i>

135
00:09:08,050 --> 00:09:11,670
<i>it could destroy not just the forest,
but the village, too.</i>

136
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:14,660
<i>It's just five seconds.</i>

137
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:16,930
<i>That's not enough time to explain.</i>

138
00:09:16,930 --> 00:09:20,530
<i>And Frieren and I haven't had the time
to establish a mutual understanding</i>

139
00:09:20,530 --> 00:09:22,940
<i>or learn to trust each other.</i>

140
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:27,120
<i>I don't even know what Frieren's thinking.</i>

141
00:09:28,650 --> 00:09:29,540
<i>That's right.</i>

142
00:09:30,360 --> 00:09:34,140
I never know what she's thinking.

143
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:41,410
I don't believe adventurers require
mutual understanding or trust.

144
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:47,280
My companion Frieren was
especially terrible at both.

145
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:53,080
So I decided to trust her words.

146
00:09:54,180 --> 00:09:58,860
She swore she would defeat the Demon King,

147
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:01,750
and I chose to believe her.

148
00:10:02,750 --> 00:10:04,550
<i>Trust her words, huh?</i>

149
00:10:11,020 --> 00:10:13,720
Don't fight it alone.

150
00:10:13,720 --> 00:10:16,280
I promise I'll defeat it.

151
00:10:17,710 --> 00:10:19,900
She looked like a mess.

152
00:10:20,860 --> 00:10:22,990
Don't blame me for whatever happens!

153
00:10:23,650 --> 00:10:25,260
Awakening spell!

154
00:10:28,450 --> 00:10:30,420
Frieren, only the core—

155
00:10:51,990 --> 00:10:54,320
Got it.

156
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:07,330
What's going on here?

157
00:11:10,170 --> 00:11:12,000
Thank you.

158
00:11:12,780 --> 00:11:14,880
I won't forget this.

159
00:11:14,880 --> 00:11:18,220
We didn't do much.

160
00:11:18,690 --> 00:11:19,330
No.

161
00:11:20,110 --> 00:11:23,220
I've never been happier to be
awakened, thanks to you.

162
00:11:28,430 --> 00:11:33,800
The moment I woke up, I knew that it
was the kind that reflects magic.

163
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:37,850
You really are an incredible mage.

164
00:11:37,850 --> 00:11:41,350
But we made it out alive
because of you, Sein.

165
00:11:42,390 --> 00:11:43,700
Thanks.

166
00:11:49,110 --> 00:11:50,300
Good boy.

167
00:11:57,350 --> 00:12:00,240
That lady from the village sure was pretty.

168
00:12:00,710 --> 00:12:02,370
You're still saying that?

169
00:12:03,870 --> 00:12:05,850
That's Vorig?

170
00:12:06,970 --> 00:12:10,560
It's the relay point to Äußerst.

171
00:12:10,560 --> 00:12:12,910
We're finally halfway there?

172
00:12:12,910 --> 00:12:15,150
This really is a long journey.

173
00:12:15,720 --> 00:12:18,700
We'll resupply in Vorig, and—

174
00:12:18,700 --> 00:12:24,060
Ms. Frieren, this is awkward,
but we're nearly out of funds.

175
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:25,690
Hey, you.

176
00:12:25,980 --> 00:12:26,810
What is it?

177
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:31,570
That's a noble's carriage.

178
00:12:32,350 --> 00:12:33,210
Huh?

179
00:12:34,640 --> 00:12:36,320
What? What's going on?

180
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:40,330
You've got a nice body.
Your appearance isn't too bad either.

181
00:12:40,740 --> 00:12:42,960
Come to my mansion.

182
00:12:43,970 --> 00:12:46,860
What's going on?

183
00:12:52,830 --> 00:12:54,390
What's your name?

184
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:56,230
Stark.

185
00:12:56,230 --> 00:12:59,690
Lord Orden, you can't do this.

186
00:12:59,690 --> 00:13:01,730
You know who I am?

187
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,520
This is the mansion of the Orden family,

188
00:13:04,520 --> 00:13:06,810
one of the Three Great Families
of the northern lands.

189
00:13:06,810 --> 00:13:09,360
Your grandfather was a high-handed man, too.

190
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:12,780
What is this nonsense?

191
00:13:12,780 --> 00:13:16,380
Either way, I'm having a conversation
with Stark right now.

192
00:13:16,690 --> 00:13:19,110
He's my party's front-liner.

193
00:13:20,890 --> 00:13:25,660
Stark, do you need your mommy's
permission to have a conversation?

194
00:13:25,660 --> 00:13:27,250
Please don't fight.

195
00:13:27,940 --> 00:13:30,880
I have a request. I'll pay you.

196
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:33,640
This is ridiculous. We're leaving, Stark.

197
00:13:33,640 --> 00:13:35,210
Ms. Frieren.

198
00:13:39,710 --> 00:13:41,660
What are those copper coins?

199
00:13:41,660 --> 00:13:43,890
They're all the funds we have left.

200
00:13:45,870 --> 00:13:48,730
I guess we can at least hear you out.

201
00:13:48,730 --> 00:13:50,420
Frieren...

202
00:13:53,690 --> 00:13:56,520
Stark, where are you from?

203
00:13:57,210 --> 00:14:00,160
A warrior village in the Klee region
of the central lands.

204
00:14:00,570 --> 00:14:04,790
I see. My family line is originally
from that village, too.

205
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:11,950
My eldest son, Wirt.

206
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:16,300
My heir and hero of this city.

207
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,140
He looks like you, Mr. Stark.

208
00:14:19,630 --> 00:14:20,980
You're practically identical.

209
00:14:21,630 --> 00:14:23,430
If we got you cleaned up,

210
00:14:23,430 --> 00:14:26,200
only someone from within the family
would be able to tell the difference.

211
00:14:26,520 --> 00:14:29,570
What does that have to do with your request?

212
00:14:30,810 --> 00:14:35,060
A month ago, we fought a
large battle against demons.

213
00:14:35,060 --> 00:14:37,880
Wirt died in action.

214
00:14:37,880 --> 00:14:40,580
He and the enemy commander
killed each other.

215
00:14:41,420 --> 00:14:46,030
The silver lining was that
it was a large-scale melee.

216
00:14:47,390 --> 00:14:51,260
The only ones who know of Wirt's death
are Gabel over there

217
00:14:51,260 --> 00:14:53,200
and a few of my most trusted men.

218
00:14:56,790 --> 00:14:59,590
What are you planning to make Stark do?

219
00:15:02,300 --> 00:15:06,740
The fortress city of Vorig is key
to this region's defense.

220
00:15:07,070 --> 00:15:11,990
We can't allow morale to fall while
we're rebuilding the forces we lost.

221
00:15:12,520 --> 00:15:15,590
So you want to cover up
your son's death until then.

222
00:15:16,440 --> 00:15:21,970
Three months from now, this region's
influential figures will meet at a soirée.

223
00:15:21,970 --> 00:15:24,500
We'll show them that Wirt is alive and well.

224
00:15:25,020 --> 00:15:26,460
How much will you pay us?

225
00:15:26,460 --> 00:15:28,500
Ten Strahl gold coins.

226
00:15:29,030 --> 00:15:32,250
That's three meals a day
plus snacks for a year.

227
00:15:32,250 --> 00:15:33,760
Throw in a grimoire.

228
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:37,000
You can take one of your
choosing from the library.

229
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,590
Hold on. I can't do this.

230
00:15:39,590 --> 00:15:41,860
What am I gonna do about
this scar on my forehead?

231
00:15:41,860 --> 00:15:46,620
The people already believe
Wirt is recuperating.

232
00:15:46,620 --> 00:15:48,860
That's an honorable battle scar.

233
00:15:51,300 --> 00:15:54,090
Mr. Stark, we need the money.

234
00:15:54,090 --> 00:15:55,350
This is sad.

235
00:15:56,130 --> 00:15:59,420
Gabel, teach Stark proper etiquette.

236
00:16:26,220 --> 00:16:29,630
Mr. Stark, how is your etiquette
training coming along?

237
00:16:29,630 --> 00:16:31,030
It's hell.

238
00:16:39,380 --> 00:16:40,600
What do you think?

239
00:16:42,450 --> 00:16:43,690
It doesn't suit you.

240
00:16:43,690 --> 00:16:44,660
That's not nice.

241
00:16:45,590 --> 00:16:48,180
I'm gonna get some fresh air.

242
00:16:56,030 --> 00:16:57,360
Lord Orden.

243
00:16:59,430 --> 00:17:02,850
Aren't I always telling you
to call me "Father"?

244
00:17:02,850 --> 00:17:05,190
You never know when someone
might be watching.

245
00:17:05,550 --> 00:17:07,550
Aren't you being too cold?

246
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:11,530
It's been less than two months
since your son died.

247
00:17:11,790 --> 00:17:15,170
I'm simply following my son's final wishes.

248
00:17:15,700 --> 00:17:17,900
I even had a double prepared.

249
00:17:18,780 --> 00:17:21,070
He didn't look as much like him
as you do, though.

250
00:17:22,730 --> 00:17:26,530
I can't say I enjoy this.

251
00:17:28,920 --> 00:17:31,490
But it's what my son wanted.

252
00:17:32,090 --> 00:17:33,700
I'll need your cooperation.

253
00:17:36,830 --> 00:17:38,860
You're so awkward.

254
00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:46,520
Mut, stand up taller.

255
00:17:46,520 --> 00:17:48,190
Yes, Father.

256
00:17:49,250 --> 00:17:50,880
My second son, Mut.

257
00:17:51,370 --> 00:17:54,860
He's to be my heir, but he
shows little improvement.

258
00:17:55,660 --> 00:17:59,460
But by contrast, Stark is a failure.

259
00:18:03,900 --> 00:18:06,870
Skill with a sword affects one's posture.

260
00:18:06,870 --> 00:18:09,580
I'll teach you the Orden family technique.

261
00:18:14,590 --> 00:18:15,890
What's wrong?

262
00:18:21,620 --> 00:18:24,030
It's all right. Take deep breaths.

263
00:18:25,840 --> 00:18:28,450
This isn't your village.

264
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:34,100
Lord Orden, what do you think of Mut?

265
00:18:34,520 --> 00:18:36,400
He doesn't have his brother's talent.

266
00:18:38,020 --> 00:18:39,670
But he's a hard worker.

267
00:18:40,850 --> 00:18:43,730
What he's worked to build up
won't let him down.

268
00:18:44,200 --> 00:18:47,790
Someday he'll become
a stronger knight than me.

269
00:18:48,210 --> 00:18:49,390
I see.

270
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:52,780
You should tell that to Mut.

271
00:18:53,050 --> 00:18:54,610
I do.

272
00:18:55,040 --> 00:18:58,160
But it's gone to his head,
so he doesn't improve.

273
00:18:59,790 --> 00:19:01,720
Now that's a problem.

274
00:19:02,460 --> 00:19:03,050
What?

275
00:19:04,220 --> 00:19:06,680
I have to learn etiquette, too?

276
00:19:06,920 --> 00:19:08,670
It's a soirée.

277
00:19:08,670 --> 00:19:12,730
You expect a young man to attend it alone?

278
00:19:14,150 --> 00:19:17,220
Or will his mommy escort him instead?

279
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:20,200
Good luck.

280
00:19:53,780 --> 00:19:56,460
This past month has been hell.

281
00:19:57,870 --> 00:19:59,620
We need the money, right?

282
00:20:00,180 --> 00:20:02,010
I'm sorry.

283
00:20:11,510 --> 00:20:14,750
We practiced. We might as well dance.

284
00:20:16,250 --> 00:20:18,630
That really doesn't suit you.

285
00:21:28,600 --> 00:21:30,070
Shall we dance as well?

286
00:21:31,380 --> 00:21:32,760
I'm going to eat cake.

287
00:21:34,830 --> 00:21:35,780
I see.

288
00:21:40,710 --> 00:21:45,720
If not for this injury,
I would've been in the vanguard.

289
00:21:46,590 --> 00:21:49,360
Perhaps then, Wirt wouldn't have died.

290
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:50,500
Milord...

291
00:21:54,950 --> 00:21:57,090
Looks like the soirée is over.

292
00:21:59,720 --> 00:22:01,350
You've completed the job.

293
00:22:01,650 --> 00:22:05,320
Gabel, give Frieren her reward.

294
00:22:13,390 --> 00:22:18,290
Stark, you're an incredibly
powerful warrior.

295
00:22:18,940 --> 00:22:20,370
If you'd like—

296
00:22:20,370 --> 00:22:23,130
I can't replace your son.

297
00:22:24,090 --> 00:22:25,190
You're right.

298
00:22:26,220 --> 00:22:29,630
And I can't replace your father.

299
00:22:30,090 --> 00:22:32,740
But you don't have a home to return to.

300
00:22:33,180 --> 00:22:36,970
The warrior village of the Klee region
was destroyed by demons.

301
00:22:36,970 --> 00:22:38,520
I know that much.

302
00:22:40,680 --> 00:22:43,050
My son and I parted in anger.

303
00:22:43,760 --> 00:22:46,030
We got into an argument
over something trivial.

304
00:22:46,690 --> 00:22:51,510
I told him I never wanted
to see his face again.

305
00:22:53,260 --> 00:22:55,350
I didn't mean it.

306
00:22:58,970 --> 00:23:02,920
When I found you,
I thought it was a miracle.

307
00:23:06,130 --> 00:23:08,690
All the more reason I can't stay here.

308
00:23:10,150 --> 00:23:12,920
I said something I didn't mean, too,

309
00:23:13,830 --> 00:23:15,520
to the man who raised me.

310
00:23:16,500 --> 00:23:21,240
So I have to bring him back
stories of my travels.

311
00:23:23,750 --> 00:23:24,930
I see.

312
00:23:30,800 --> 00:23:33,820
Which grimoire should
I choose for my reward?

313
00:23:34,080 --> 00:23:37,350
Ms. Frieren, it's been half a day.

314
00:23:38,210 --> 00:23:39,950
You're distressing Mr. Gabel.

315
00:23:40,730 --> 00:23:42,040
Please hurry up.

316
00:23:42,770 --> 00:23:45,630
Look. All the life has left him.

317
00:23:46,700 --> 00:23:50,310
It'll be some time until
they rebuild their forces.

318
00:23:53,870 --> 00:23:54,980
What is it?

319
00:23:57,290 --> 00:23:58,260
Nothing.

320
00:24:02,330 --> 00:24:06,180
At the very least, he won't
have to worry about his heir.

321
00:24:20,780 --> 00:24:22,880
A forgotten hero, huh?

322
00:24:21,740 --> 00:24:25,230
He's a forgotten hero.
No one even knows his name.

323
00:24:23,190 --> 00:24:24,730
He's totally decrepit.

324
00:24:23,980 --> 00:24:26,000
He's been alone this whole time.

325
00:24:25,450 --> 00:24:27,970
Humans grow quickly.

326
00:24:27,970 --> 00:24:30,110
He's a long-lived friend of mine.
